<!DOCTYPE html><html><head><title>IRC channel logs</title><style>html {
  background: #fdfdfd;
}

h1 {
  font-weight: 300;
}

h2 {
  font-weight: 200;
}

h3 {
  padding: .5em 0;
  border-top: 3px dotted #ddd;
  margin-bottom: 0;
}

form {
  width: 400px;
  display: flex;
}

input {
  width: 100%;
  display: flex;
  border-radius: .25em 0 0 .25em;
  border: 1px solid #aaa;
  border-right: 0;
  padding: 0.5em;
}

button {
  display: flex;
  border-radius: 0 .25em .25em 0;
  background-color: #007bff;
  border: 1px solid #007bff;
  padding: .5em;
  cursor: pointer;
  color: white;
}

button:hover {
  background-color: #0069d9;
  border-color: #0062cc;
}

a {
  color: #007bff;
  text-decoration: none;
}

a:hover {
  text-decoration: underline;
}

h4 {
  margin-bottom: .5em;
}

table td {
  padding: 0.75em;
}

table tr:hover {
  background: #eee;
}

.year {
  display: table;
}

.month {
  display: table-cell;
  padding-right: 1em;
}

ul {
  margin: 0;
  padding: 0;
  list-style: none;
}

.nick {
  padding-right: 0.6rem;
  font-weight: bold;
  text-align: right;
  width: 13rem;
  display: table-cell;
}

.nick a {
  color: inherit;
  text-decoration: none;
}

.message {
  display: table-cell;
  padding-left: 0.6rem;
  border-left: 2px solid #333;
}

.notice {
  color: #859900;
  font-style: italic;
}

.line {
  line-height: 1.8rem;
  display: table;
}

#logs {
  margin-top: 1.5rem;
  padding: 1.5rem;
}
</style></head><body><h1>IRC channel logs</h1><h2>2025-01-05.log</h2><p><a href="/bootstrappable">back to list of logs</a></p><div id="logs"><div class="line" id="000613"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#000613" label="[00:06:13]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">fossy: IMO the right way to &quot;solve&quot; it is via posix-runner</span></div><div class="line" id="000719"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#000719" label="[00:07:19]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yeah..</span></div><div class="line" id="000752"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#000752" label="[00:07:52]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">adding paging support to builder-hex0 seems like a fool's errand</span></div><div class="line" id="000904"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#000904" label="[00:09:04]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&amp; there's not much else you can do besides paging to deal with a memory hole @ 1GiB</span></div><div class="line" id="001012"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#001012" label="[00:10:12]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and then, you have the issue of moving to Fiwix</span></div><div class="line" id="001032"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#001032" label="[00:10:32]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">where the initrd needs to be contiguous in physical RAM</span></div><div class="line" id="001130"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#001130" label="[00:11:30]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Mikaku: Fiwix doesn't support anything like an initramfs, right?</span></div><div class="line" id="001339"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#001339" label="[00:13:39]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">if it did, we could skip the ext2 ramdisk creation and just hand the builder-hex0 ramfs over to Fiwix and have it parse it into a proper initramfs</span></div><div class="line" id="001534"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#001534" label="[00:15:34]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well, isn't the ext2 ramdisk ~= initramfs?</span></div><div class="line" id="001620"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#001620" label="[00:16:20]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">no, it's initrd</span></div><div class="line" id="001648"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#001648" label="[00:16:48]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it's backed by a block device backed by RAM</span></div><div class="line" id="001713"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#001713" label="[00:17:13]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">vs an initramfs, which has no block device - basically just a tmpfs that's prefilled</span></div><div class="line" id="001718"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#001718" label="[00:17:18]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">ah, okay</span></div><div class="line" id="001913"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#001913" label="[00:19:13]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">hmmmmmm, that would be very useful</span></div><div class="line" id="002007"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#002007" label="[00:20:07]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">hmm, would not be straightforward though...</span></div><div class="line" id="002105"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#002105" label="[00:21:05]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">For a start, it would mean not having to allocate a full 1GiB+ contiguous memory block for a ramdisk initially only holding a couple hundred MiB</span></div><div class="line" id="002223"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#002223" label="[00:22:23]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">And this is a real issue for UEFI as well - we might not have a 1GiB+ contiguous block at all</span></div><div class="line" id="002250"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#002250" label="[00:22:50]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">A tmpfs or initramfs can be discontiguous, in theory</span></div><div class="line" id="002329"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#002329" label="[00:23:29]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and it can grow or shrink dynamically</span></div><div class="line" id="002841"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#002841" label="[00:28:41]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">a RAMdisk could be discontiguous in theory as well</span></div><div class="line" id="002904"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#002904" label="[00:29:04]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">clearly builder-hex0 doesn't support that, but i assume Fiwix does</span></div><div class="line" id="003724"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#003724" label="[00:37:24]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well, we do need to add paging support to posix-runner though</span></div><div class="line" id="003738"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#003738" label="[00:37:38]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but at least it should be easier</span></div><div class="line" id="003743"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#003743" label="[00:37:43]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">than with builder-hex0</span></div><div class="line" id="003814"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#003814" label="[00:38:14]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and it's not just about 1 GiB contiguous block</span></div><div class="line" id="003905"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#003905" label="[00:39:05]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it's also about the fact that we request some memory in fiwix, even if it is 1 GiB contigous block, it might not be the right block that we want</span></div><div class="line" id="012354"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#012354" label="[01:23:54]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yes, if we were to link anything between builder-hex0 and fiwix we would have to coerce/patch fiwix into reserving a particular block</span></div><div class="line" id="013309"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#013309" label="[01:33:09]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">anyway, we are nowhere close to building fiwix there...</span></div><div class="line" id="013328"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#013328" label="[01:33:28]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">first of all, even x86_64 userspace bootstrap doesn't work and tcc-mes is buggy</span></div><div class="line" id="013821"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#013821" label="[01:38:21]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">if the route mes-&gt;tcc,busybox-stuff is feasible, i can fiddle together tccboot+linux24/romfs</span></div><div class="line" id="013913"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#013913" label="[01:39:13]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">although i didn't continue testing tccboot/jit, but i do recall i had once pulled in some recent libtcc version into tccboot, sufficient for linux-2.4.37.11 for nosmp boot with extended hardware-support</span></div><div class="line" id="013956"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#013956" label="[01:39:56]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">tccboot itself however does need binutils for 16bit asm</span></div><div class="line" id="014104"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#014104" label="[01:41:04]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">this might need a re-integration of binutils due to autotools involved, but oasis-linux already had some lua-scripting for this which can be used as template</span></div><div class="line" id="014740"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#014740" label="[01:47:40]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i don't know at which phase any build-time dependency needed the fiwix/linux2 abi - and binutils(detangled from autotools) must be compiled without the presence of fiwix/linux2x</span></div><div class="line" id="022528"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#022528" label="[02:25:28]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">tccboot could be utilized for fiwix too; in any case a 16bit assembler is needed _before_ any such kernel abi</span></div><div class="line" id="022651"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#022651" label="[02:26:51]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">if 16bit bootcode was rewritten, as86 assembler would introduce a less excessive dependency graph than binutils-as would</span></div><div class="line" id="074404"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#074404" label="[07:44:04]">&lt;Mikaku&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Googulator: yes, Fiwix supports initrd as long as you pass it as part of a Multiboot spec v1 data</span></div><div class="line" id="074500"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#074500" label="[07:45:00]">&lt;Mikaku&gt;</a></span><span class="message">under QEMU would be: qemu-system-i386 ... -append &quot;root=/dev/ram0 ramdisksize=nnn initrd=initrd.img&quot; -initrd initrd.img</span></div><div class="line" id="075019"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#075019" label="[07:50:19]">&lt;Mikaku&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Googulator: ah, now I read you meant a dynamic ramdisk (like tmpfs). No this is not supported</span></div><div class="line" id="075040"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#075040" label="[07:50:40]">&lt;Mikaku&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I think that putting the buffer cache on top of the current ramdisk driver could be a solution to avoid having to preallocate 1GiB of contiguous memory</span></div><div class="line" id="134300"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#134300" label="[13:43:00]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">is it possible to bootstrap a binutils-as (when detangled from autotools) and/or as86 assembler for 16bit bootcode asm processing, without the presence of fiwix/linux2 ABI as build-time dependency?</span></div><div class="line" id="200913"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#200913" label="[20:09:13]">&lt;mwette&gt;</a></span><span class="message">ludocode: I stumbled across here.   I was reading the onramp description and wondered what you do about alignment of data and how that maps to restrictions on machine hardware loads and stores.</span></div><div class="line" id="201806"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#201806" label="[20:18:06]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">that probably only matters if you try to optimize things?</span></div><div class="line" id="204526"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#204526" label="[20:45:26]">&lt;mwette&gt;</a></span><span class="message">some machines will raise exception if you try to load a 32 bit data from a 16 bit aligned address</span></div><div class="line" id="212726"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#212726" label="[21:27:26]">&lt;mwette&gt;</a></span><span class="message">from the riscv-unpriv spec: &quot;loads and stores that are not naturally aligned may either complete execution successfully or raise an exception&quot;</span></div><div class="line" id="214355"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#214355" label="[21:43:55]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">hmm, I don't think I saw this in practice...</span></div><div class="line" id="214408"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#214408" label="[21:44:08]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">stage0-riscv64 seems to be running fine</span></div><div class="line" id="214436"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#214436" label="[21:44:36]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and variables in .text are not aligned there</span></div><div class="line" id="214602"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#214602" label="[21:46:02]">&lt;mwette&gt;</a></span><span class="message">The behavior would be specific to processor implementation, what processor are you running on?</span></div><div class="line" id="214914"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#214914" label="[21:49:14]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">jh7110</span></div><div class="line" id="214926"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#214926" label="[21:49:26]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">(visionfive2)</span></div><div class="line" id="220011"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#220011" label="[22:00:11]">&lt;mwette&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Thx.  I'm familiar w/ vf2 (have one but it's not working right now).  I was just looking through qemu source to see if they have a switch for the behavior.</span></div><div class="line" id="221940"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#221940" label="[22:19:40]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">oh, that might be useful to know</span></div><div class="line" id="221952"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#221952" label="[22:19:52]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">for now I keep using older qemu</span></div><div class="line" id="222005"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#222005" label="[22:20:05]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">since on newer qemu stage0-posix-riscv* exibit random crashes</span></div><div class="line" id="222044"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#222044" label="[22:20:44]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">at some point I should try to bisect git revisions between versions 8.0 and 8.1...</span></div><div class="line" id="222103"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#222103" label="[22:21:03]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">still, it's probably something else</span></div><div class="line" id="223559"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#223559" label="[22:35:59]">&lt;mwette&gt;</a></span><span class="message">riscv/translate.c(gen_jal) has code to gen execption on misaligned jal</span></div><div class="line" id="225124"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#225124" label="[22:51:24]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">oh, but that's not so easy to get misaligned</span></div><div class="line" id="225201"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#225201" label="[22:52:01]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">since all the instructions have the same length (32-bits)</span></div><div class="line" id="225218"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#225218" label="[22:52:18]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">so you have to do something very deliberately to misalign instructions</span></div><div class="line" id="225403"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#225403" label="[22:54:03]">&lt;mwette&gt;</a></span><span class="message">right.  I didn't anticipate branches would be an issue.</span></div><div class="line" id="225518"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#225518" label="[22:55:18]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I think the most realistic thing to get misaligned are some stored variables at the end of the file</span></div><div class="line" id="225533"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#225533" label="[22:55:33]">&lt;mwette&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Check target/riscv/tcg/tcg-cpu.c.  There is a config that sets do_unaligned_access.</span></div><div class="line" id="225534"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#225534" label="[22:55:34]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i.e. assembly equivalent of global variables</span></div><div class="line" id="225628"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#225628" label="[22:56:28]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yeah, I can see it</span></div><div class="line" id="225800"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#225800" label="[22:58:00]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">also, jal can only address  16-byte chunks if I remember correctly</span></div><div class="line" id="225816"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#225816" label="[22:58:16]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">let me check</span></div><div class="line" id="230354"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#230354" label="[23:03:54]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yes, for J type instructions immediate[0] is not encoded</span></div><div class="line" id="230414"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#230414" label="[23:04:14]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">so jumps are automatically even</span></div><br /></div></body></html>